{
"After Parmenides, every serious theory kept some basic stuff that is never created or destroyed. The same principle reappears in the modern laws of conservation of matter and energy.": "bfc4e79eac8c",
"Anaxagoras Nous, a mind that orders matter but is mixed with nothing, was the first clear distinction between mind and matter. The atomists went the other way, explaining everything by bodies in motion. The two options still divide philosophers of mind.": "b4a81ed7dba0",
"Argued in a poem that what is not cannot be, and concluded that change, motion, and plurality are illusions. His argument set the problem for everyone after him.": "1f76025f8cdc",
"Atoms move through the void.": "5041daba811d",
"Brought philosophy to Athens and taught that a portion of everything is in everything, set in motion by Nous, mind. He was prosecuted for saying the sun is a hot stone.": "2b4e7dd9e0f2",
"Democritus said that sweet and bitter exist by convention.": "45025a4ef449",
"For Parmenides, change was an illusion of the senses.": "dcbb3b1500d9",
"For thou canst not know what is not—that is impossible—nor utter it; for it is the same thing that can be thought and that can be.": "0ba1f2643b21",
"Founder of a community that studied number, music, and the soul. His followers held that the principles of mathematics are the principles of all things.": "1f126a173171",
"Heraclitus of Ephesus, writing around 500 BCE, made change itself the key. In John Burnets translation: You cannot step twice into the same rivers; for fresh waters are ever flowing in upon you. For Heraclitus the world is an ever-living fire, and its stability is like the stability of a flame or a river, a pattern kept up by constant change. He also thought opposites depend on each other. Cold things become warm, and what is warm cools; what is wet dries, and the parched is moisted. When Homer prayed that strife might perish, Heraclitus said, he did not see that he was praying for the destruction of the universe. The lesson on the river in the Big Questions shelf takes up his puzzle in detail.": "7e6f6e6544e9",
"Heraclitus saw the world as a state of constant flux.": "f1485e110247",
"Leucippus and his associate Democritus say that the full and the empty are the elements, calling the one being and the other non-being.": "b19278e5e37c",
"Parmenides denied that real plurality exists.": "db43af6c0c5c",
"Parmenides of Elea, in southern Italy, wrote a poem in which a goddess teaches him the only two ways of search that can be thought of. In Burnets translation, the first is that It is, and that it is impossible for it not to be. The second, that It is not, is a path that none can learn of at all. For thou canst not know what is not—that is impossible—nor utter it; for it is the same thing that can be thought and that can be. From this he drew startling conclusions. If what is not cannot be, then nothing can come into being, since it would have to come from nothing. Nothing can perish, since it would have to become nothing. There can be no empty space, no motion, and no real plurality. The world of change that our senses show us is an illusion.": "5675b8fd2682",
"Parmenides showed that a chain of reasoning could reach a conclusion that contradicts everything we see, and that the conclusion must be answered with better reasoning, not with a shrug. Philosophy has argued this way ever since.": "0c0908640038",
"Proposed four eternal roots, earth, water, air, and fire, mixed and separated by Love and Strife. The four elements lasted in European science until the seventeenth century.": "cd9d155c2e10",
"Pythagoras moved from Samos to southern Italy around 530 BCE and founded a community that was part school and part religious order. Its members discovered that musical harmony depends on simple ratios of string lengths, and they generalized boldly. In W. D. Rosss translation of Aristotles Metaphysics, the Pythagoreans thought its principles were the principles of all things, meaning the principles of mathematics, because in numbers they seemed to see many resemblances to the things that exist. The Milesians had looked for a stuff. The Pythagoreans looked for a structure. It was the first appearance of an idea that modern physics still takes seriously, that the world is written in mathematics.": "d687648283c0",
"The atomists rescued change without breaking Parmenides rule.": "0cb2b877ddac",
"The conservation of matter echoes the rule that nothing comes from nothing.": "1b0cddd95f7f",
"The most lasting answer came from Leucippus and his student Democritus of Abdera. In Rosss translation, Aristotle reports that they say that the full and the empty are the elements, calling the one being and the other non-being. The full is made of atoms, from the Greek for uncuttable, tiny solid bodies that differ only in shape, arrangement, and position. The empty is the void in which they move. Atoms never come into being or perish, which satisfies Parmenides, but they move and combine, which rescues change. Everything else, color, taste, warmth, exists only by convention. The atomists were not doing experiments, and their atoms are not the atoms of chemistry. But the idea that the visible world is made of invisible particles in empty space, governed by necessity and not by purpose, would return two thousand years later as the foundation of modern science.": "b715c0eceef7",
"The next generation took the second route. Empedocles of Acragas, in Sicily, accepted that nothing comes from nothing, but said there are four eternal roots, which we call earth, water, air, and fire. In Burnets translation: Hear first the four roots of all things. They never come into being or perish. They only mix and separate, moved by two forces, Love and Strife. At one time all the limbs that are the bodys portion are brought together by Love, and at another they are severed by cruel Strife. Anaxagoras of Clazomenae, who brought philosophy to Athens, went further. Every kind of stuff is in everything, in tiny portions: All things were together, infinite both in number and in smallness. What sets the mixture moving is Nous, mind, which is infinite and self-ruled, and is mixed with nothing.": "24be52237ebe",
"The word atom means indivisible, or uncuttable.": "93b2423a6ab6",
"There is no coming into being of aught that perishes, nor any end for it in baneful death; but only mingling and change of what has been mingled.": "71030fe9a83e",
"This was the first sustained argument in Western philosophy, and it was purely logical. Parmenides did not appeal to observation. He followed a premise to its conclusion and accepted the conclusion even though it contradicted everything we see. His student Zeno defended him with paradoxes, such as Achilles who can never catch the tortoise, designed to show that motion leads to absurdity, and the lesson on Zenos paradoxes examines them. Most philosophers since have rejected Parmenides conclusion. But they could not simply ignore his argument. If you want change, you have to show where the reasoning goes wrong, or find a way for things to change without anything coming from nothing.": "3736163650ce",
"With his teacher Leucippus, explained everything as atoms moving in the void. Colors and tastes, he said, exist by convention, and only atoms and the void exist in truth.": "27004455b070",
